When we talk about the assumptions of the t-test, two key ideas are the "distribution" and "variability" of your data. The distribution refers to how your data points are spread out—specifically, the t-test assumes that the data in each group roughly follow a normal (bell-shaped) distribution. Variability means how much the data points differ from each other within each group; the t-test assumes the groups have similar variability, called equal variances.

To check these assumptions with your tissue sample data in MATLAB, you can start by visualizing the data. Plotting histograms or boxplots for each group helps you see if the data look roughly symmetric and bell-shaped or if there are outliers or skewness. For variability, you can compare the spread of the data in each group using measures like standard deviation or variance.

MATLAB also has functions to test these assumptions more formally. For example, you can use `kstest` or `lillietest` to check normality and `vartest2` to test if the variances are equal between groups. These tests give you p-values too, which tell you if the assumptions are likely violated.

Here’s a small example of how you might check normality and variance equality for two groups `group1` and `group2`:

```matlab
% Check normality
[h1, p1] = lillietest(group1);
[h2, p2] = lillietest(group2);

% Check equal variances
[h_var, p_var] = vartest2(group1, group2);

fprintf('Group1 normality p-value: %.3f\n', p1);
fprintf('Group2 normality p-value: %.3f\n', p2);
fprintf('Equal variances p-value: %.3f\n', p_var);
```

If these p-values are above 0.05, the assumptions are generally considered met. If not, you might need to consider alternative tests or data transformations.

Try plotting your data and running these tests to see how your tissue sample data behave. This will give you a clearer picture of whether the t-test assumptions hold.

To help you interpret histograms and boxplots for checking normality, here’s a scaffold for how to create and start reading these plots in MATLAB. I’ll show you how to make a histogram for one group of your tissue samples, which will let you see the shape of the data distribution visually.

```matlab
% Example: Create a histogram for one group of tissue sample data
group1_data = [your_data_here]; % replace with your actual data vector
figure;
histogram(group1_data);
title('Histogram of Group 1 Tissue Samples');
xlabel('Measurement Value');
ylabel('Frequency');
```

This histogram shows how many data points fall into different value ranges (bins). For a normal distribution, you want to see a roughly symmetric, bell-shaped curve centered around the average value. If the bars are skewed heavily to one side, or if there are multiple peaks or extreme outliers, that suggests the data may not be normally distributed.

You can also create a boxplot to summarize the spread and identify outliers:

```matlab
figure;
boxplot(group1_data);
title('Boxplot of Group 1 Tissue Samples');
ylabel('Measurement Value');
```

In a boxplot, the box shows the middle 50% of the data (between the 25th and 75th percentiles), the line inside the box is the median, and the “whiskers” extend to the rest of the data except for outliers, which are shown as individual points. A symmetric boxplot with no extreme outliers is another sign your data might be approximately normal.

This scaffold covers how to create and start interpreting these plots for one group. You’ll want to do this for each group you’re comparing. What do your histograms and boxplots look like when you try this with your data?

